1
00:00:27,608 --> 00:00:33,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a a quote from a Ajahn Annāmalai Thera, you might know.

2
00:00:36,312 --> 00:00:47,217
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, keep a watchful eye on the gateway, not acting out of pressure that endures on any level, regardless of how long it endures.

3
00:00:49,598 --> 00:00:55,381
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what will make you an island for yourself, make you an island for yourself.

4
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:09,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to recap, to go through it again.

5
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:16,695
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the gateway that one must be guarding?

6
00:01:17,756 --> 00:01:24,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Correctly established mindfulness on that peripheral level, for which

7
00:01:25,353 --> 00:01:28,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue and sense restraint are necessary prerequisites.

8
00:01:29,855 --> 00:01:32,717
[SPEAKER_01]: As we said in previous talks, you won't even see.

9
00:01:33,657 --> 00:01:35,058
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't even know how to see.

10
00:01:35,358 --> 00:01:51,006
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not appear for you, the, you know, the domain of the peripheral, unless your behavior has been purified beforehand, unless you have been cultivating virtue and sense restraint and watchfulness and being moderate in eating and so on.

11
00:01:52,027 --> 00:01:52,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when you

12
00:01:53,600 --> 00:02:08,486
[SPEAKER_01]: When you try to apply this, like, okay, I'll learn how to... well, I'll learn how to recognize what's also there, in my experience, right here, right now, such as this body, for example, without needing to look at it, to know that it's there, because you already know that it's there.

13
00:02:09,026 --> 00:02:10,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, how would you know what to look for?

14
00:02:13,668 --> 00:02:18,490
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the dependence on that direct seeing as a measure of existence,

15
00:02:19,397 --> 00:02:23,618
[SPEAKER_01]: And putting that peripheral knowledge of what's already there existing, second.

16
00:02:24,619 --> 00:02:27,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's learning how to reverse that order, fix it up.

17
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:31,961
[SPEAKER_01]: You do that, you guide that, that's the gateway.

18
00:02:33,381 --> 00:02:35,242
[SPEAKER_01]: That right order, right mindfulness.

19
00:02:37,222 --> 00:02:39,823
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, okay, so you're guiding that right order.

20
00:02:43,704 --> 00:02:46,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in a way that you said that the gate, the gateway,

21
00:02:48,405 --> 00:02:53,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that mental choosing to engage with unwholesome things?

22
00:02:54,726 --> 00:03:01,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, yeah, perspective for your choice is the perspective of the right order.

23
00:03:02,488 --> 00:03:05,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the right order is the gateway.

24
00:03:05,288 --> 00:03:14,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you choose whether to pervert the order and give in to the pressure and, you know, put the second as first.

25
00:03:14,970 --> 00:03:15,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example,

26
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:39,602
[SPEAKER_01]: You mindful of the body correctly on the peripheral level, as the gateway, your virtue has been cultivated beforehand and so on, and now you have, you know, some doubts about some things regarding the body, regarding this, but all of that, all of those doubts, mental states, hindrances, pressures, are within the body enduring there on the peripheral level.

27
00:03:40,138 --> 00:03:47,043
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is never a reason to abandon that and put it second and prioritize that which doubt presented you with.

28
00:03:48,904 --> 00:03:54,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's choosing to abandon the gateway when you choose to accept what doubt presented you with.

29
00:03:56,190 --> 00:04:00,473
[SPEAKER_01]: You walk away from it and that's the whole point of doubt as a hindrance.

30
00:04:01,914 --> 00:04:06,377
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the whole... the whole trick of it is just to get you to walk away from your post.

31
00:04:11,002 --> 00:04:17,244
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you can never... that's why the right answers will never satisfy that doubt, because there was never a point.

32
00:04:17,344 --> 00:04:18,365
[SPEAKER_01]: There was just a pretext.

33
00:04:19,985 --> 00:04:30,129
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless you're able to withstand the pressure of the doubt and sustain the right order in face of it, you will not be able to endure it.

34
00:04:33,551 --> 00:04:38,873
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see, whatever hindrance will arise, it all arises within.

35
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:40,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

36
00:04:40,790 --> 00:05:00,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Hindrance tries to throw you off, pressure you, pull you, scare you, frighten you, all these things, just so you abandon the post of the right order, just so you drop, or obscure, or walk away from the recognition of peripherally enduring body there.

37
00:05:01,974 --> 00:05:05,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it makes you engage with...

38
00:05:05,414 --> 00:05:06,574
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not making you engage.

39
00:05:07,335 --> 00:05:09,715
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pressuring you to, and you choose to, yeah.

40
00:05:10,399 --> 00:05:14,523
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you are responsible for the... if hindrances hinder you.

41
00:05:15,123 --> 00:05:20,528
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not responsible for hindrances arising, but you're responsible if you're hindered by them.

42
00:05:20,568 --> 00:05:24,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it means like, oh, you accepted the order that they were pressuring you for.

43
00:05:25,113 --> 00:05:26,714
[SPEAKER_01]: You acted out of them, in other words.

44
00:05:28,936 --> 00:05:30,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see that order, correct?

45
00:05:31,358 --> 00:05:35,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You endure the order in face of the pressure of the hindrances.

46
00:05:35,641 --> 00:05:36,081
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

47
00:05:36,762 --> 00:05:38,583
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you go surmount the hindrances.

48
00:05:38,863 --> 00:05:39,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Surmounting the hindrances.

49
00:05:40,024 --> 00:05:40,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Not resolving them.

50
00:05:41,105 --> 00:05:42,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Not finding the right answers for them.

51
00:05:43,066 --> 00:05:48,390
[SPEAKER_00]: But isn't it... you see a hindrance there, pressuring you.

52
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:53,613
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can also see that you can choose to engage with it.

53
00:05:54,133 --> 00:05:55,034
[SPEAKER_00]: You can choose to give in.

54
00:05:55,514 --> 00:05:55,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

55
00:05:56,915 --> 00:05:58,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you see the power of your choice.

56
00:05:59,817 --> 00:06:00,798
[SPEAKER_00]: The power you have there.

57
00:06:02,023 --> 00:06:02,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

58
00:06:02,223 --> 00:06:04,124
[SPEAKER_00]: You can either give in or not.

59
00:06:04,225 --> 00:06:09,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but that power of your choice, it's not rooted just in, like, the you or something.

60
00:06:10,649 --> 00:06:12,170
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in the options.

61
00:06:12,190 --> 00:06:12,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

62
00:06:12,570 --> 00:06:13,651
[SPEAKER_01]: But that option is there.

63
00:06:13,791 --> 00:06:13,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

64
00:06:14,271 --> 00:06:20,176
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the option is to stay at the gateway and not prioritize the hindrance or to prioritize the hindrance.

65
00:06:21,456 --> 00:06:24,218
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, what's really the power there?

66
00:06:24,539 --> 00:06:25,239
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the choice.

67
00:06:25,559 --> 00:06:26,940
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the knowledge behind it.

68
00:06:27,421 --> 00:06:29,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, the choice is a form of power.

69
00:06:29,122 --> 00:06:29,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

70
00:06:29,562 --> 00:06:29,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowledge.

71
00:06:30,223 --> 00:06:30,843
[SPEAKER_01]: What to do?

72
00:06:31,947 --> 00:06:47,196
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know, you must stay at the gateway, despite all the pressure, despite all the unnatural feeling of it, because it feels just much more natural and satisfying to address this and fix it and sort it, this pressure of the doubt or whatever else.

73
00:06:48,176 --> 00:06:53,219
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless you have thoroughly understood that, no, that's a trap, you won't do it.

74
00:06:53,779 --> 00:06:59,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't accidentally or spontaneously choose to guard the right order and become a sotāpanna.

75
00:06:59,303 --> 00:07:00,023
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

76
00:07:00,930 --> 00:07:07,836
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to understand the value through pondering on it beforehand and then stick to it and endure it against the grain, against the pull.

77
00:07:10,278 --> 00:07:12,239
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what the world's telling you, you know.

78
00:07:12,359 --> 00:07:12,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

79
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:16,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whatever the world's telling you, you have a reference point now.

80
00:07:17,003 --> 00:07:21,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if what the world's telling you is according to the right order, you say, okay, the world is right in this instance.

81
00:07:21,467 --> 00:07:24,489
[SPEAKER_01]: If the world is telling you the opposite, you say, no, the world is wrong in this instance.

82
00:07:32,095 --> 00:07:50,379
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... so that, again, guarding, you say, you're saying, guarding that gateway, is not letting those hindrances in, not engaging with them.

83
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:53,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's really not engaging with them.

84
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:53,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Not engaging.

85
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:56,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Because letting them in might be misconstrued.

86
00:07:57,441 --> 00:07:58,021
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what you mean.

87
00:07:58,662 --> 00:08:00,482
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what I meant, probably, if I said it before,

88
00:08:04,130 --> 00:08:07,511
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be misconstrued, like, oh, I must prevent them from coming to me.

89
00:08:08,971 --> 00:08:09,251
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

90
00:08:09,311 --> 00:08:13,633
[SPEAKER_01]: They arise, they come to the doorstep, you just don't let them in.

91
00:08:13,693 --> 00:08:14,533
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you let them in?

92
00:08:14,553 --> 00:08:17,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you welcome them, you delight in them, you accept them.

93
00:08:17,954 --> 00:08:18,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't do that.

94
00:08:20,175 --> 00:08:31,698
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, as I said so many times before, you don't try to chase them away either, because that would also require you to abandon your post at the very threshold of the gateway.

95
00:08:32,325 --> 00:08:37,227
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to go out and start chasing them and so on, which means, you know, you turn your back to that which you should have guarded.

96
00:08:44,730 --> 00:08:53,993
[SPEAKER_01]: So, endure the pressure to let them in, if they are agreeable, and endure the pull to chase them away, if they are disagreeable.

97
00:08:56,334 --> 00:08:58,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And endure the pull to ignore them, if they are neither.

98
00:09:04,759 --> 00:09:06,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because look, they're not trying to come in.

99
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:07,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I can snooze at the post.

100
00:09:07,821 --> 00:09:08,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

101
00:09:08,481 --> 00:09:08,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

102
00:09:09,122 --> 00:09:10,102
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they're not trying to come in.

103
00:09:10,122 --> 00:09:10,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

104
00:09:10,943 --> 00:09:11,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not...

105
00:09:11,543 --> 00:09:18,948
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have to endure the the pull of chasing them away and the pressure of trying to... of them trying to get in, but I certainly should not go to sleep either.

106
00:09:19,628 --> 00:09:25,432
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... it's relaxing when they're not trying to get in and they're just sitting there, but at the same time,

107
00:09:26,045 --> 00:09:27,225
[SPEAKER_01]: I shouldn't be too relaxed.

108
00:09:27,545 --> 00:09:32,807
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where there's the subtlest, the neutral feeling that basically needs to be understood.

109
00:09:33,147 --> 00:09:38,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just so easy to become indolent and careless about it.

110
00:09:38,408 --> 00:09:39,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Complacent.

111
00:09:39,208 --> 00:09:40,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Distracted from.

112
00:09:40,268 --> 00:09:40,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

113
00:09:42,329 --> 00:09:45,370
[SPEAKER_00]: So, vigilant in times of calm.

114
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:45,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

115
00:09:46,290 --> 00:09:46,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

116
00:09:46,550 --> 00:09:52,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Vigilant in times of calm and then persistent in the time.

117
00:09:52,972 --> 00:09:53,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's the

118
00:09:55,148 --> 00:09:58,151
[SPEAKER_01]: pressure to welcome it, you persist in not doing so.

119
00:09:59,072 --> 00:10:08,881
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's the pull to take you, pull you out, to chase them away, if they're un... un... un... if they're disagreeable, you also have to persist in that, enduring that pull.

120
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:29,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, see, that that's a wrong question, because it already implies that you start engaged.

121
00:10:30,268 --> 00:10:30,729
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't.

122
00:10:31,951 --> 00:10:36,877
[SPEAKER_01]: You start pressured or pulled, but that's not the engagement.

123
00:10:38,139 --> 00:10:40,923
[SPEAKER_01]: The engagement is what you do on account of that pressure that you feel.

124
00:10:48,313 --> 00:10:53,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Is to not act out of that feeling and develop sufficient mindfulness in regard to it peripherally and endure it.

125
00:10:56,059 --> 00:10:58,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, and also see the value in doing it.

126
00:10:58,921 --> 00:11:00,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, that needs to come beforehand.

127
00:11:01,062 --> 00:11:04,425
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't start doing this without still doubting the value of doing it.

128
00:11:05,306 --> 00:11:06,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this kind of question comes up...

129
00:11:13,124 --> 00:11:14,404
[SPEAKER_01]: People understand this.

130
00:11:14,444 --> 00:11:20,806
[SPEAKER_01]: People are pressured by the pain, but they want the answer to get rid of the pain, which means they keep doing the same thing, acting out of pain.

131
00:11:21,886 --> 00:11:26,187
[SPEAKER_01]: But the gradual training is there, repeated so many times in the suttas and in our talks.

132
00:11:27,628 --> 00:11:34,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, virtue is the first, guarding of the sense doors, sense restraint, moderation.

133
00:11:35,650 --> 00:11:36,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Do that.

134
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:39,270
[SPEAKER_01]: All the doubt that comes, well, restrain it.

135
00:11:40,031 --> 00:11:40,731
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

136
00:11:41,689 --> 00:11:46,053
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the point to find the satisfying answer over and over and over again.

137
00:11:46,413 --> 00:11:50,978
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is to endure the non-answering on the basis of your virtue and mindfulness.

138
00:11:52,159 --> 00:11:53,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you just give in.

139
00:11:53,700 --> 00:12:02,168
[SPEAKER_01]: You build this up, you're coming near the gateway, and then you just run out, chasing the answers and so on, and forget where you should have stopped and protected it.

140
00:12:02,868 --> 00:12:05,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it keeps coming up, because people are impatient.

141
00:12:09,206 --> 00:12:27,702
[SPEAKER_01]: You always want someone to tell you what you must do, but they tell you what to do, so... And then you want to be told again what you need to do, because the satisfaction of the answer has run out, and the principle of acting out of pressure or the pull hasn't diminished, hasn't changed.

142
00:12:27,742 --> 00:12:28,562
[SPEAKER_01]: You still act out.

143
00:12:29,643 --> 00:12:30,904
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't practiced that yet.

144
00:12:30,924 --> 00:12:32,646
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't practiced that.

145
00:12:32,686 --> 00:12:35,188
[SPEAKER_01]: All you've been doing is acting out and finding answers.

146
00:12:35,618 --> 00:12:37,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, you have to start with.

147
00:12:37,579 --> 00:12:41,901
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, the point comes when you have sufficient amount of answers.

148
00:12:42,341 --> 00:12:44,202
[SPEAKER_01]: You have sufficient amount of information.

149
00:12:44,762 --> 00:12:46,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you need to start applying it.

150
00:12:46,863 --> 00:12:52,365
[SPEAKER_01]: And enduring that doubt, that's going to become more and more irrational, the more you don't give in to it.

151
00:12:54,506 --> 00:12:57,047
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's going to be more and more pressuring, so to speak.

152
00:12:57,107 --> 00:13:02,109
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you have the virtue, you have the sense restraint, got it, and you endure it.

153
00:13:02,189 --> 00:13:03,469
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will have to calm down.

154
00:13:03,569 --> 00:13:05,070
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind will have to calm down.

155
00:13:18,768 --> 00:13:23,951
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was reading about the development of the perception of abandoning.

156
00:13:24,051 --> 00:13:26,813
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of perception must be developed.

157
00:13:28,734 --> 00:13:32,416
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's pretty much similar to what you're saying here.

158
00:13:33,096 --> 00:13:33,957
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just read it.

159
00:13:34,457 --> 00:13:35,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And which is the perception of

160
00:13:44,631 --> 00:13:44,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

161
00:13:44,791 --> 00:13:47,392
[SPEAKER_00]: This is called the perception of death.

162
00:13:47,412 --> 00:13:47,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

163
00:13:47,893 --> 00:13:48,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and that's often, see,

164
00:14:10,585 --> 00:14:14,628
[SPEAKER_01]: People read just that, and mean like, oh, I must prevent these things from arising.

165
00:14:16,369 --> 00:14:17,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I must get rid of them.

166
00:14:18,071 --> 00:14:20,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So, these thoughts come, and I must have them not come.

167
00:14:21,954 --> 00:14:31,321
[SPEAKER_01]: And the result of it is, you abandon the gateway, you abandon the mindfulness, you abandon the right order, and you welcome, and you engage, and you start fighting or chasing

168
00:14:33,768 --> 00:14:35,109
[SPEAKER_01]: which is what they wanted you to do.

169
00:14:35,149 --> 00:14:37,330
[SPEAKER_01]: They wanted you to abandon the gateway and accept them.

170
00:14:37,810 --> 00:14:44,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to try to abandon thoughts, you actually accepted them and abandoned that which you shouldn't have abandoned, which is the right order of your right mindfulness.

171
00:14:45,395 --> 00:14:52,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can understand abandonment to mean that, but you can also understand abandonment to mean what we are saying it means.

172
00:14:53,279 --> 00:14:59,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you abandon the thoughts, you abandon these wild animals, they will stop coming to you if you stop feeding them.

173
00:15:01,142 --> 00:15:02,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's endless.

174
00:15:02,263 --> 00:15:05,467
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start chasing them and killing them one by one, they keep popping back up.

175
00:15:06,268 --> 00:15:06,668
[SPEAKER_01]: New ones.

176
00:15:06,708 --> 00:15:09,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't, you know, you can cancel the thought.

177
00:15:10,293 --> 00:15:11,314
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is that now?

178
00:15:11,394 --> 00:15:12,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free from thoughts forever?

179
00:15:13,176 --> 00:15:17,201
[SPEAKER_01]: You're free from underlying currency of like the pressuring feeling in regard?

180
00:15:17,261 --> 00:15:17,461
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

181
00:15:17,781 --> 00:15:18,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing has changed.

182
00:15:18,842 --> 00:15:20,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You just got rid of the specific thought that bothered you.

183
00:15:27,245 --> 00:15:38,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, after the abandonment, or if you understand the abandonment the way I'm describing it, then you realize, okay, so you actually unplug, uproot, remove that fuel for these thoughts to come back.

184
00:15:38,227 --> 00:15:39,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, yeah, you abandon them.

185
00:15:40,928 --> 00:15:44,129
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just one instance of it, but now they stop coming.

186
00:15:44,489 --> 00:15:45,629
[SPEAKER_01]: You're no longer feeding them.

187
00:15:45,769 --> 00:15:47,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, because you stop feeding them.

188
00:15:47,709 --> 00:15:50,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, by not welcoming, you will abandon them.

189
00:15:50,370 --> 00:15:54,631
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how monks skillfully abandon these thoughts and abandon their unskillful states.

190
00:15:57,395 --> 00:16:03,220
[SPEAKER_00]: But particularly this last point, he does not give in to the continual arising of evil.

191
00:16:03,560 --> 00:16:05,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because it'll stop coming.

192
00:16:05,502 --> 00:16:05,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

193
00:16:06,162 --> 00:16:07,724
[SPEAKER_00]: And they are continually arising.

194
00:16:07,764 --> 00:16:25,198
[SPEAKER_01]: They're continually arising, he's not giving in, the order is not compromised, the right order, the mindfulness established on the body peripherally correctly, that gateway is not abandoned, doesn't matter how continually these thoughts arise, this does not change.

195
00:16:26,333 --> 00:16:33,939
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the arahant... well, the one who practices is steadfast in that, and, you know, it basically... it becomes fortified in that right order.

196
00:16:35,060 --> 00:16:42,506
[SPEAKER_01]: The more that's developed, the less and less these thoughts will be coming, so to speak.

197
00:16:43,047 --> 00:16:47,090
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be fewer and fewer and fewer, and the pressure will be fading away.

198
00:16:47,510 --> 00:16:53,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Because each time you abandon the gateway, you engage with the thoughts, which means that's what they feed on.

199
00:16:53,255 --> 00:16:55,877
[SPEAKER_01]: They see they can move you, they'll keep coming to try and move you.

200
00:16:56,955 --> 00:17:05,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But if after a sufficient amount of time, the order hasn't moved, they will basically drift away, lose interest and walk away, see that they've been wasting their time.

201
00:17:06,247 --> 00:17:11,273
[SPEAKER_00]: But in this last thing, he doesn't give in to the continual arising.

202
00:17:11,894 --> 00:17:12,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma is saying...

203
00:17:24,899 --> 00:17:40,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Continuously, right, right, right, right.

204
00:17:40,846 --> 00:17:42,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's exactly what happens, yeah.

205
00:17:42,367 --> 00:17:50,171
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're still engaging with the thoughts individually and, you know, trying to chase them away, get rid of them, when it's one or two, you do it successfully.

206
00:17:50,191 --> 00:17:50,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

207
00:17:50,831 --> 00:17:53,272
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, exactly, three or four, you can't keep up

208
00:17:53,872 --> 00:17:55,213
[SPEAKER_01]: 15, 16, oh, you're done.

209
00:17:55,433 --> 00:18:02,077
[SPEAKER_01]: If that was the only way of abandonment that you knew, get rid of, get rid of, get rid of, you'll be overwhelmed, overrun, quickly.

210
00:18:02,498 --> 00:18:05,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that might work for one thought.

211
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:05,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

212
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:18,188
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly why people struggle, because it kind of works initially, and they think, oh, so that's the abandonment they must do, but they don't realize that abandonment that you must do is basically on the level of fortifying where you stand.

213
00:18:19,137 --> 00:18:22,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Fighting more and more thoughts at the same time, because that's impossible.

214
00:18:22,639 --> 00:18:23,499
[SPEAKER_01]: That's going to run out.

215
00:18:23,580 --> 00:18:25,941
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically going to be exhausting, and you're going to be run over.

216
00:18:26,981 --> 00:18:29,583
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be fortified to that, no matter how...

217
00:18:30,103 --> 00:18:33,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is one thought, and you can just ignore it, of the unwholesome kind.

218
00:18:33,305 --> 00:18:38,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, abandon it in their way, but don't confuse that for the uprooting of it.

219
00:18:39,168 --> 00:18:40,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, I don't want to think about this.

220
00:18:40,889 --> 00:18:44,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's fine if it works, but the time will come when that will not work.

221
00:18:44,491 --> 00:18:47,133
[SPEAKER_01]: And if there's the only way of abandoning that you knew,

222
00:18:47,888 --> 00:18:54,613
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to be overrun, they'll be inside, you'll be running out, the gateway is gone, destroyed, you know, precepts compromised, and everything else.

223
00:18:56,114 --> 00:19:11,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to... people have to basically broaden the understanding of what abandonment is, and certainly stop conflating it with, I must stop thinking and get rid of this, because even the thought of abandoning this particular thought, is another thought.

224
00:19:12,446 --> 00:19:14,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking that you must stop thinking,

225
00:19:15,140 --> 00:19:22,127
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not abandonment at all, and it's actually a contradiction in terms, which means, as such, it's quite a wrong view.

226
00:19:22,147 --> 00:19:29,874
[SPEAKER_01]: And often you see that in Buddhist meditations and traditions of how to stop thinking, stop thinking, focus now, feel the world.

227
00:19:30,074 --> 00:19:33,217
[SPEAKER_01]: All of these focusing and feeling and stuff are thoughts, are perceptions.

228
00:19:33,698 --> 00:19:36,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, here you are believing you could stop your thoughts.

229
00:19:39,144 --> 00:19:45,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just that that's not the abandonment, you're actually kind of not even seeing the nature of that which is in front of your gate.

230
00:19:46,727 --> 00:19:48,128
[SPEAKER_01]: You're completely confused about it.

231
00:19:49,588 --> 00:19:50,489
[SPEAKER_01]: You think it's not real.

232
00:19:50,509 --> 00:19:51,229
[SPEAKER_01]: You think it's not there.

233
00:19:51,249 --> 00:19:52,029
[SPEAKER_01]: These thoughts are gone.

234
00:19:52,450 --> 00:19:55,671
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind is empty of thoughts, which is another thought.

235
00:19:55,691 --> 00:20:01,913
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see the thoughts are always being there, and the only way you can do is protect the gateway in regard to it.

236
00:20:06,744 --> 00:20:20,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if there are no thoughts, evil, unwholesome thoughts, you're still developing and keeping that perception of abandoning there.

237
00:20:20,935 --> 00:20:21,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

238
00:20:21,415 --> 00:20:24,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And what would that keeping be?

239
00:20:25,538 --> 00:20:26,939
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be sustaining of the order.

240
00:20:27,100 --> 00:20:28,120
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what it is.

241
00:20:28,441 --> 00:20:34,205
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said before, sustaining of the right order, peripheral mindfulness of the body being there,

242
00:20:35,243 --> 00:20:40,865
[SPEAKER_01]: means everything else that you're experiencing within that, is going to be experienced in a detached manner.

243
00:20:42,246 --> 00:20:44,827
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, whatever feeling he feels, he feels it detached.

244
00:20:48,008 --> 00:20:49,389
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's exactly what it is.

245
00:20:49,409 --> 00:20:51,890
[SPEAKER_01]: You protect that, that's the abandonment of any attachment.

246
00:20:53,030 --> 00:20:55,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandonment on any base for passion.

247
00:20:55,832 --> 00:21:03,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sustain it until the greed, aversion, delusion and any traces of it completely evaporate.

248
00:21:03,715 --> 00:21:04,195
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

249
00:21:10,735 --> 00:21:12,516
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that confusing, really.

250
00:21:12,636 --> 00:21:18,238
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that confusing, obviously, but exactly that's the point, because it is the right practice.

251
00:21:19,418 --> 00:21:23,139
[SPEAKER_01]: The closer you get to it, the more will your mind try to throw from it.

252
00:21:23,539 --> 00:21:24,420
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Mahārāja is.

253
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:30,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And your own mind is the, you know, an assassin that pretends to be a friend, as the sūtas say.

254
00:21:31,842 --> 00:21:36,104
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you start doing it rightly, the stronger the doubts will be.

255
00:21:36,984 --> 00:21:39,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, the more of the fortifying

256
00:21:39,681 --> 00:21:41,843
[SPEAKER_01]: of the right order you have to do and sustain.

257
00:21:42,424 --> 00:21:43,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Or faith you have to have.

258
00:21:43,946 --> 00:21:44,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

259
00:21:44,566 --> 00:21:45,687
[SPEAKER_01]: That basic reason.

260
00:21:45,707 --> 00:21:46,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

261
00:21:46,689 --> 00:21:48,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the faith is kind of prerequisite, isn't it?

262
00:21:57,019 --> 00:22:00,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, you know, you could see that perception.

263
00:22:06,952 --> 00:22:09,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Those unwholesome things, I don't have to choose them.

264
00:22:10,114 --> 00:22:11,454
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to engage with those things.

265
00:22:13,295 --> 00:22:14,436
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you abandon them?

266
00:22:14,536 --> 00:22:15,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so I don't have to do anything, really.

267
00:22:16,457 --> 00:22:18,698
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have to go out of my way to abandon them.

268
00:22:19,138 --> 00:22:22,540
[SPEAKER_01]: I just have to stay here and not go anyway, basically.

269
00:22:22,660 --> 00:22:24,501
[SPEAKER_01]: And they will be abandoned.

270
00:22:25,041 --> 00:22:31,104
[SPEAKER_01]: By not being given attention to, and acted out of, and welcomed, and delighted, and engaged, and so on.

271
00:22:31,204 --> 00:22:31,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Encouraged.

272
00:22:32,164 --> 00:22:32,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Encouraged, yeah.

273
00:22:33,891 --> 00:22:34,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Not invited.

274
00:22:34,672 --> 00:22:37,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Not invited, all of that.

275
00:22:39,516 --> 00:22:41,918
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's difficult to do, obviously, because it's a very simple thing.

276
00:22:41,938 --> 00:22:50,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because that's basically now on the level of enduring it mentally, that discomfort of that... You know, waiting.

277
00:22:50,685 --> 00:22:52,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Waiting, patience in front of you, like,

278
00:22:53,038 --> 00:23:03,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to pressure you, trying to convince you, oh, do this, do that, let them in, go out, or just one slip, you get careless, oh, oh, that's it, that's it, you acted out.

279
00:23:03,266 --> 00:23:07,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, that's that's the pain of the practice, but pain of the development.

280
00:23:07,970 --> 00:23:14,815
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you take it on, that's also the pain that results in uprooting of any basis for pain in the future.

281
00:23:15,455 --> 00:23:17,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not just managing anymore.

282
00:23:23,143 --> 00:23:24,224
[SPEAKER_01]: And then another thought comes.

283
00:23:24,924 --> 00:23:27,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so I have to chase it away again, which means I'm not really free.

284
00:23:28,446 --> 00:23:31,448
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm pressured by the duty of having to chase it away, having to deal with it.

285
00:23:32,249 --> 00:23:42,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And then too many thoughts come, and you can't deal with it, because you only knew how to deal with individual thoughts, by kind of addressing them, changing them, chasing them away.

286
00:23:44,697 --> 00:23:49,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, management will never result in freedom, only in postponing the obvious.

287
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:52,061
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are still equally pressured.

288
00:23:52,862 --> 00:24:01,589
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as you said, as I said in that quote, it is what will make you an island for yourself.

289
00:24:01,609 --> 00:24:02,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

290
00:24:02,709 --> 00:24:04,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Consistent vigilance.

291
00:24:05,111 --> 00:24:10,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely, basically, self-sufficient internally, contained, detached.

292
00:24:12,257 --> 00:24:14,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Highlands detached from any other.

293
00:24:14,418 --> 00:24:16,180
[SPEAKER_00]: From the beginning, it's the same.

294
00:24:20,667 --> 00:24:24,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Fortifying the same principle.

295
00:24:29,113 --> 00:24:34,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's exactly the gist of every gradual training that the Buddha described, which is always the same gradual training.

296
00:24:35,157 --> 00:24:38,560
[SPEAKER_01]: But every instance he described, it's always the same principle.

297
00:24:39,821 --> 00:24:47,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Take it on, do this, do this, do this, and then dwell in these states, and protect these states, and so on, and there's only one outcome.

298
00:24:47,286 --> 00:24:48,487
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be an island to yourself.

